Description: antinomie: add the arm64 debug engine
 The engine's arm64 side was a stub that offered nothing. It now debugs
 AArch64 programs as the x86-64 side does: an AArch32 program on the same
 guest is still refused.
 .
 A software breakpoint is a BRK with an immediate only the engine plants
 (0xa71; a program's own BRK keeps its SIGTRAP), taken from
 do_el0_brk64() before the signal. A single step is the hardware step
 (TIF_SINGLESTEP: MDSCR_EL1.SS with SPSR.SS), armed on the stopped task
 as ptrace arms it and taken from do_el0_softstep(); the saved SS bit is
 cleared again once the step is done. A stepped SVC ends at the system
 call's exit: syscall_trace_exit() asks the engine before ptrace's step
 report. Neither reaches the program as SIGTRAP or ptrace state.
 .
 Hardware breakpoints and watchpoints use the perf breakpoints (BRPs and
 WRPs, up to four of each), and a watchpoint may watch loads alone
 (ANTC_DEBUG_BP_HW_READ, offered with CAPS READ_WATCH of revision 20).
 arm64 takes both before the instruction or its access completes, so a
 thread resumed where one stopped it runs that instruction with its debug
 registers off and one step armed, as the kernel steps a suspended
 breakpoint; the step's exception (or the SVC's exit) turns them on again.
 .
 Registers are NT_PRSTATUS (user_pt_regs), NT_PRFPREG (user_fpsimd_state)
 and NT_ARM_TLS. x86-64 is unchanged: the arm64 pieces of debug.c and of
 the shared headers are compiled only there.
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-01

